{
 "about": "dev.tinhat.invite_rules: how every Tin Hat side reads the `v` of a person's rule for invites from other servers (server: Synapse module tinhat_network.parse_rule; the apps copy this file byte for byte). Each case's content_json is the account data's JSON text (null = no account data); parse it with your own JSON parser, then read it.",
 "type": "dev.tinhat.invite_rules",
 "read": {
  "accepted_v": "a JSON number whose correctly rounded double (IEEE 754 binary64, round-to-nearest) is exactly 1 (1, 1.0, 1e0, 0.99999999999999999, ...: after JSON parsing these are the same value in JavaScript; a Rust reader needs serde_json's float_roundtrip feature or another correctly rounding parser), or the string \"1\" or \"1.0\" exactly (after JSON unescaping; no spaces or other digits); never a boolean",
  "rejected_v": "nobody: any other v, or no v, in a document that has other keys. Never looser than what the person stored, whatever they stored",
  "no_document": "known (the default): no account data, or an empty JSON object (Tin Hat's way to clear the setting; Matrix can't delete account data)",
  "rule_word": "with an accepted v: remote = exactly \"anyone\", \"known\" or \"nobody\"; anything else = known (unchanged)",
  "unreadable": "nobody, like a rejected v: a document that isn't a JSON object, isn't valid JSON, or holds anywhere a number whose double isn't finite (1e400, a 400-digit integer), whatever your parser does with it"
 },
